# Target C — Section 128: no strict-rhombus train returns to one bounded face

**Date:** 2026-09-12
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**
**Status:** proof-only exact structural theorem.
**Dependencies:** canonical positive-core/force-hull normalization and relative-interior KKT equilibrium; Section 127 only for the harmonic-cell corollary.

Let `G=(V,C)` be the nonisolated positive-contact core of the canonical post-ZF
representative, equipped with a relative-interior normalized KKT stress. Thus every
positive contact has weight `alpha_e>0`, and every active diameter edge has positive
weight. Let

\[
B=\operatorname{Vert}(F)
\]

be the loaded force-hull vertex set.

A nontrivial strict-rhombus train has a simple midpoint path through successive rhombi,
meeting the contact graph exactly at the midpoints of its parallel train edges. The
claim is

\[
\boxed{
\text{the two terminal sides of a nontrivial train cannot belong to one bounded face.}
}
\tag{128.1}
\]

## 128.1 Close the train through the bounded face

Assume the two terminal contact edges `e_-`, `e_+` are incident, on their terminal
sides, to the same bounded face `K` of `G`.

Join their midpoints by a simple arc inside the open face `K`, avoiding the finitely
many contact-isolated source points. Together with the train midpoint path this gives a
simple closed curve `Gamma` crossing the positive-contact graph transversely exactly at
the train contact edges.

The train path lies in the convex hull of positive-core vertices, hence in `F`. The
bounded face `K` also lies in `F`. Therefore

\[
\Gamma\subset F,
\qquad
\operatorname{int}(\Gamma)\subseteq\operatorname{conv}(\Gamma)\subseteq F.
\tag{128.2}
\]

The Jordan interior is open in the plane. No loaded vertex can lie there: every
`b in B` is an extreme boundary point of `F`, while membership in the open Jordan
interior would give a planar neighborhood of `b` contained in `F`.

Hence the bounded shore `S` of the cut contains no loaded vertex and therefore no
endpoint of an active diameter edge. In particular,

\[
\delta_H(S)=\varnothing.
\tag{128.3}
\]

## 128.2 The contact cut has a nonzero resultant

Orient the common train-edge direction `u` so every contact crossing `Gamma` points
from the exterior shore into `S`. Train geometry makes this orientation coherent along
the whole midpoint path. The contact cut is exactly the train-edge set:

\[
\delta_C(S)=E(\tau).
\tag{128.4}
\]

All relative-interior contact weights are positive, so

\[
A_\tau:=\sum_{e\in E(\tau)}\alpha_e>0.
\]

The total contact-force resultant entering `S` is therefore

\[
A_\tau u\ne0.
\tag{128.5}
\]

## 128.3 Summed equilibrium contradicts the cut

Sum KKT equilibrium over all positive-core vertices in `S`. Internal contact forces
cancel pairwise. Internal diameter forces cancel pairwise, and (128.3) says no diameter
edge crosses the cut. The only surviving boundary forces are the train contacts, so
summed equilibrium requires

\[
A_\tau u=0,
\]

contradicting (128.5).

Thus (128.1) holds exactly and finitely.

## 128.4 Harmonic-cell consequence

Section 127 identifies every bounded face of every harmonic induced graph `H_r` with a
unique bounded face of the full positive-contact core. Therefore a complete local
strict-rhombus train cannot have both terminal physical occurrences on the same bounded
local defect face.

Equivalently, in the endpoint multigraph whose vertices are stopping faces, every
bounded-face vertex is loop-free. A loop may still occur at the outer stopping face.

This does not reduce the scalar zero-host count by itself: different bounded faces can
all have host displacement zero. The gain is topological—every bounded-to-bounded
train transports between two distinct actual inactive chambers.

## 128.5 Scope

The theorem does not exclude outer-to-outer trains, outer-to-bounded trains, or trains
joining two distinct bounded faces. It uses no finite shear, no affine-kernel statement
on contact-isolated coordinates, and no old response theorem. Contact-isolated source
points remain in the full source and can be avoided by the auxiliary closing arc.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only exact train-loop exclusion. C-II remains open.